{"content":[{"signature":"","thinking":"\nLet me analyze this task carefully.\n\nTask SE-PE-01: Grammar-guided node reduction on a parse tree.\n\nKey rules from the candidate_artifact:\n1. Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes (process deeper nodes first)\n2. Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\n3. Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\n4. Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\n5. Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order\n\nLet me understand the algorithm:\n- We have a tree of nodes\n- Each node has: id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility\n- We have required_symbols that must remain represented in the retained nodes\n- We want to reduce the tree by removing nodes\n\nThe algorithm (Perses-style reduction):\n1. Process nodes from deepest to shallowest (and by node ID for ties)\n2. For each node, try to remove it:\n   - Can only remove if `removable` is True\n   - Cannot remove if it would leave a required symbol unrepresented\n   - Cannot remove if any of its children are still retained\n   - If removal is valid, remove it\n3. Track removed nodes in order and retained nodes\n\nLet me think about \"retained children\" - a node can be removed only if it has no retained children. This means we process bottom-up.\n\nWait, let me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nSo a node cannot be removed if:\n1. It's the root (parent is null)\n2. Its symbol is in required_symbols\n3. It has retained children\n\nAnd tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented.\n\nThe order of processing: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" with \"node ID as stable tie break at equal depth\".\n\nSo we sort nodes by (depth descending, node_id ascending) and try to remove each.\n\nLet me trace through example 0:\nNodes:\n- root: depth 0, symbol \"root\", removable false\n- n1: depth 1, symbol \"s1\", removable false\n- n2: depth 1, symbol \"s2\", removable true\n- n3: depth 1, symbol \"s0\", removable false\n- n4: depth 2, symbol \"s1\", parent n2, removable true\n- n5: depth 2, symbol \"s2\", parent n3, removable false\n- n6: depth 2, symbol \"s0\", parent n1, removable true\n\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nSymbols present: root(root), s1(n1, n4), s2(n2, n5), s0(n3, n6)\n\nProcessing order (depth desc, id asc):\ndepth 2: n4, n5, n6\ndepth 1: n1, n2, n3\ndepth 0: root\n\nProcess n4 (depth 2, symbol s1, removable true, parent n2):\n- removable: yes\n- not root\n- symbol s1 is in required_symbols\n- Wait: \"Never remove... a required symbol\" - does this mean a node whose symbol is required, or does it mean we can't remove a node if doing so would remove the last instance of a required symbol?\n\nI think \"a required symbol\" means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But that seems too strict - what if there are multiple nodes with the same required symbol?\n\nLet me re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nAnd separately: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nSo the constraints are:\n1. Node must be removable (removable=True)\n2. Node is not root\n3. The node's symbol is not a required symbol (OR: after removal, all required symbols are still represented?)\n\nActually, I think there are two separate constraints that together mean:\n- Hard constraint: node.removable must be True\n- Hard constraint: node is not root (redundant with removable=False for root)\n- Hard constraint: \"not a required symbol\" - I think this means the node's symbol is not in required_symbols... but then combined with \"all required symbols remain represented\" it could mean we can only remove if the symbol remains represented elsewhere\n\nActually, let me re-read more carefully: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nI think \"a required symbol\" here means a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. So we can NEVER remove any node whose symbol is in required_symbols, regardless of other copies.\n\nBut wait, that conflicts with \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" - if we can never remove required symbol nodes, why would we need to check if all required symbols remain represented?\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. Maybe \"a required symbol\" means we can't remove the LAST node with a required symbol. So \"a required symbol\" refers to the concept, not the node.\n\nActually, re-reading: the three constraints for \"never remove\" are:\n1. the root\n2. a [node with a] required symbol [that would be the last such node]\n3. a node with retained children\n\nThen the additional check: \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" - this operationalizes constraint 2.\n\nActually I think the simplest interpretation:\n- \"Never remove a required symbol\" = never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols (absolute ban)\n- \"Tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented\" = additionally check that after removal, at least one node with each required symbol still exists\n\nBut if we never remove nodes with required symbols, then required symbols will always be represented (since we never remove them). The second constraint would be redundant.\n\nUnless: a node's symbol is required, but the node itself has `removable=True`. Then \"never remove a required symbol\" would prevent its removal even though it's marked removable.\n\nSo the interpretation would be:\n- Never remove node if: not removable, OR is root, OR symbol in required_symbols, OR has retained children\n- Also check: after tentative removal, all required symbols still represented (this is actually automatically satisfied if we never remove required symbol nodes)\n\nWait, but then why is the second constraint listed separately? Let me think again...\n\nMaybe the first list (\"never remove root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children\") is the filtering step, and then after that filtering, we check \"all required symbols remain represented\" as an additional oracle check.\n\nActually, I think the most natural interpretation is:\n- \"a required symbol\" = a node that is THE ONLY instance of a required symbol\n- If there are multiple nodes with symbol \"s1\", you can remove one of them as long as at least one remains\n\nThis makes \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" non-redundant.\n\nLet me re-trace with this interpretation:\n\nExample 0:\nrequired_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]\n\nNodes with \"root\": just \"root\" (depth 0)\nNodes with \"s1\": n1 (depth 1), n4 (depth 2)\n\nProcessing order (depth desc, id asc): n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root\n\nProcess n4 (depth 2, symbol s1, removable true, parent n2):\n- removable: yes\n- has retained children? n4 is at depth 2, no children currently\n- After removal: s1 still represented by n1? Yes. root still represented by root? Yes.\n- → Remove n4. removal_order = [n4]\n\nProcess n5 (depth 2, symbol s2, removable false):\n- removable: NO → skip, retain n5\n\nProcess n6 (depth 2, symbol s0, removable true, parent n1):\n- removable: yes\n- has retained children? No\n- After removal: s1 still represented? Yes (n1). root still represented? Yes.\n- → Remove n6. removal_order = [n4, n6]\n\nProcess n1 (depth 1, symbol s1, removable false):\n- removable: NO → skip, retain n1\n\nProcess n2 (depth 1, symbol s2, removable true):\n- removable: yes\n- has retained children? n4 was removed, so no retained children for n2? Wait, n4 was removed. So n2 has no retained children.\n- After removal: required symbols still represented? root by root, s1 by n1. Yes.\n- → Remove n2. removal_order = [n4, n6, n2]\n\nProcess n3 (depth 1, symbol s0, removable false):\n- removable: NO → skip, retain n3\n\nProcess root (depth 0, parent null, removable false):\n- removable: NO (and it's root) → skip, retain root\n\nFinal:\nretained: root, n1, n3, n5\nremoved: n4, n6, n2 (in that order)\n\nLet me verify: \n- retained_node_ids (sorted): [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\n- removed_node_ids (in removal order): [\"n4\", \"n6\", \"n2\"]\n\nNow let me think about what \"sorted retained IDs\" means. Probably alphabetically/lexicographically? Or by some other order?\n\n\"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\"\n\nI'll sort retained IDs lexicographically.\n\nNow let me implement this:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Build node lookup\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Track retained set (start with all nodes retained)\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    \n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removed_order = []\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Must be removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Must not be root\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        \n        # Must have no retained children\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Check if all required symbols still represented after removal\n        # Tentatively remove\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        \n        # Check required symbols\n        symbols_present = set(node_map[r][\"symbol\"] for r in retained)\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_present):\n            # Restore\n            retained.add(nid)\n            continue\n        \n        # Removal is valid\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n```\n\nWait, I need to double-check the \"never remove a required symbol\" constraint. Looking at the candidate artifact again: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nI'm interpreting \"a required symbol\" as a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. But then \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented\" is the dynamic check.\n\nActually wait - if \"never remove a required symbol\" means \"never remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols,\" that would be a hard constraint independent of how many other nodes have that symbol. Let's test this interpretation against the fixtures.\n\nIn example 0, n4 has symbol \"s1\" which is in required_symbols. Under this interpretation, n4 can never be removed.\n\nThen:\n- n4: symbol s1, in required_symbols → never remove\n- n5: removable false → never remove\n- n6: removable true, symbol s0, not required → try to remove. After removal, required symbols: root (root), s1 (n1, n4). Still present. → Remove n6.\n- n1: removable false → never remove\n- n2: removable true, symbol s2, not required. No retained children (n4 is retained but... n4 is a child of n2). Wait! n4 is retained, so n2 has a retained child. → Cannot remove n2.\n- n3: removable false → never remove\n- root: removable false → never remove\n\nFinal retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5\nRemoved: n6\n\nHmm, that seems like very little reduction. Let me reconsider.\n\nI think the more sensible interpretation (for a Perses-style reducer) is:\n- The dynamic check \"all required symbols remain represented\" IS the constraint about required symbols\n- \"Never remove a required symbol\" is just a shorthand for \"never remove the last node with a required symbol\"\n\nThis is consistent with how Perses works: you can remove a subtree as long as the \"interestingness\" (here, required symbols represented) is maintained.\n\nLet me go with my original implementation. Let me verify it makes sense:\n\nFor n4 in example 0: symbol s1, parent n2, removable true. After removing n4, s1 is still represented by n1. So we can remove n4. ✓\n\nFor n2 in example 0 (after n4 is removed): symbol s2, parent root, removable true. n2's children: only n4 which was removed. So no retained children. After removing n2, required symbols: root (root), s1 (n1). Still present. → Remove n2. ✓\n\nThis makes more sense as a reduction algorithm.\n\nBut wait, let me reconsider the \"never remove a required symbol\" constraint one more time. The text says:\n\n\"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\"\n\nIn context of a grammar-guided reduction, \"a required symbol\" likely refers to a symbol (grammar terminal/non-terminal) that must remain in the output, not just a node. So if \"s1\" is required, we need at least one node with symbol \"s1\" to remain. We can remove a specific node with symbol \"s1\" as long as another one remains.\n\nThis is consistent with \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\"\n\nOK, my implementation looks correct. Let me also think about edge cases:\n\n1. What if a node is removable=True but its symbol is in required_symbols? Under my interpretation, we check dynamically. If it's the only node with that symbol, we can't remove it.\n\n2. The \"stable tie break\" at equal depth uses node ID. I'm using `n[\"id\"]` as the key, which gives lexicographic order: \"n1\" \u003c \"n2\" \u003c ... \"n9\" \u003c \"root\" (since 'n' \u003c 'r'). Wait, but \"root\" has depth 0, so it won't compete with n1-n6 for tie-breaking purposes.\n\nActually, let me check: is the sort key `n[\"id\"]` correct for alphabetical/lexicographic order? \"n1\", \"n2\", \"n3\", \"n4\", \"n5\", \"n6\" are all at depth 1 or 2. For same depth, n1 \u003c n2 \u003c n3 \u003c n4 \u003c n5 \u003c n6 lexicographically. That seems right.\n\nLet me also handle the ValueError for invalid cases:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    if \"nodes\" not in case or \"required_symbols\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required fields\")\n    \n    nodes = case[\"nodes\"]\n    required_symbols = set(case[\"required_symbols\"])\n    \n    # Validate structure\n    node_ids = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    for n in nodes:\n        if \"id\" not in n or \"parent\" not in n or \"symbol\" not in n or \"depth\" not in n or \"removable\" not in n:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Node missing required fields: {n}\")\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None and n[\"parent\"] not in node_ids:\n            raise ValueError(f\"Node {n['id']} has unknown parent {n['parent']}\")\n    \n    # Build node lookup\n    node_map = {n[\"id\"]: n for n in nodes}\n    \n    # Build children map\n    children = {n[\"id\"]: [] for n in nodes}\n    for n in nodes:\n        if n[\"parent\"] is not None:\n            children[n[\"parent\"]].append(n[\"id\"])\n    \n    # Track retained set (start with all nodes retained)\n    retained = set(n[\"id\"] for n in nodes)\n    \n    # Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\"depth\"], n[\"id\"]))\n    \n    removed_order = []\n    \n    for node in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = node[\"id\"]\n        \n        # Must be removable\n        if not node[\"removable\"]:\n            continue\n        \n        # Must not be root (parent is None)\n        if node[\"parent\"] is None:\n            continue\n        \n        # Must have no retained children\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\n            continue\n        \n        # Tentatively remove and check if all required symbols remain represented\n        retained.remove(nid)\n        \n        symbols_present = set(node_map[r][\"symbol\"] for r in retained)\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_present):\n            # Restore\n            retained.add(nid)\n            continue\n        \n        # Removal is valid\n        removed_order.append(nid)\n    \n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    \n    return {\n        \"removed_node_ids\": removed_order,\n        \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted\n    }\n```\n\nLet me trace through all three examples to verify.\n\n**Example 0:**\nNodes: root(depth 0), n1(depth 1, s1, removable F), n2(depth 1, s2, removable T), n3(depth 1, s0, removable F), n4(depth 2, s1, parent n2, removable T), n5(depth 2, s2, parent n3, removable F), n6(depth 2, s0, parent n1, removable T)\nrequired_symbols: {root, s1}\n\nsorted_nodes (depth desc, id asc):\ndepth 2: n4, n5, n6\ndepth 1: n1, n2, n3\ndepth 0: root\n\nInitial retained: {root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5, n6}\n\nn4: removable T, parent n2 (not root), children of n4: none. Tentatively remove n4.\nretained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5, n6}\nsymbols: root(root), s1(n1), s2(n2,n5), s0(n3,n6)\nrequired {root, s1} ⊆ {root, s1, s2, s0} ✓\n→ Remove n4. removed_order = [n4]\n\nn5: removable F → skip\n\nn6: removable T, parent n1 (not root), children of n6: none. Tentatively remove n6.\nretained = {root, n1, n2, n3, n5}\nsymbols: root(root), s1(n1), s2(n2,n5), s0(n3)\nrequired {root, s1} ⊆ present ✓\n→ Remove n6. removed_order = [n4, n6]\n\nn1: removable F → skip\n\nn2: removable T, parent root (not root itself), children of n2: [n4]. n4 not in retained. No retained children. Tentatively remove n2.\nretained = {root, n1, n3, n5}\nsymbols: root(root), s1(n1), s2(n5), s0(n3)\nrequired {root, s1} ⊆ present ✓\n→ Remove n2. removed_order = [n4, n6, n2]\n\nn3: removable F → skip\n\nroot: removable F, parent None → skip (both conditions)\n\nFinal retained: {root, n1, n3, n5}\nretained_sorted: [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\nremoved_order: [\"n4\", \"n6\", \"n2\"]\n\n**Example 1:**\nSame structure as example 0, same required_symbols.\nThe logic doesn't depend on utility values (I don't see utility being used in the reduction algorithm as described).\n\nWait... does utility play a role? Let me re-read the candidate_artifact:\n\n\"Reduce a registered parse tree through grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates while preserving an interestingness oracle.\"\n\nThe utility might be the interestingness oracle score, but looking at the algorithm description, it doesn't mention using utility for ordering or decisions. The ordering is purely by depth (desc) then ID (asc).\n\nHmm, but \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth\" - this suggests we only use node ID for ties in depth, not utility.\n\nSo utility doesn't affect the algorithm? Let me proceed without it.\n\n**Example 1:** Same structure, same processing order and decisions → same result.\nretained_sorted: [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\nremoved_order: [\"n4\", \"n6\", \"n2\"]\n\n**Example 2:** Same structure as well.\nretained_sorted: [\"n1\", \"n3\", \"n5\", \"root\"]\nremoved_order: [\"n4\", \"n6\", \"n2\"]\n\nAll three examples have the same tree structure and required_symbols, just different utility values. Since utility isn't used in the algorithm, they should all have the same result.\n\nMy implementation looks correct. Let me finalize it.\n\nActually, one more thought: the \"interestingness oracle\" in the real Perses algorithm involves actually compiling/running code. Here, the problem says \"parser generation and real compiler execution are excluded.\" The interestingness constraint is reduced to: \"all required symbols remain represented.\" This is what I've implemented.\n\nLet me also double-check: \"never remove... a node with retained children\" - my check `any(c in retained for c in children[nid])` checks this correctly. It uses the current state of `retained` at the time of processing each node, which is correct since we process bottom-up and already removed some children.\n\nThe implementation looks solid. Let me write the final JSON response.\n","type":"thinking"},{"text":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if \\\"nodes\\\" not in case or \\\"required_symbols\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required fields: nodes or required_symbols\\\")\\n\\n    nodes = case[\\\"nodes\\\"]\\n    required_symbols = set(case[\\\"required_symbols\\\"])\\n\\n    # Validate node structure\\n    node_ids = set()\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        for field in (\\\"id\\\", \\\"parent\\\", \\\"symbol\\\", \\\"depth\\\", \\\"removable\\\"):\\n            if field not in n:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Node missing field '{field}': {n}\\\")\\n        node_ids.add(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None and n[\\\"parent\\\"] not in node_ids:\\n            raise ValueError(f\\\"Node {n['id']} references unknown parent {n['parent']}\\\")\\n\\n    # Build lookup structures\\n    node_map = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: n for n in nodes}\\n\\n    # children[nid] = list of direct child IDs\\n    children = {n[\\\"id\\\"]: [] for n in nodes}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        if n[\\\"parent\\\"] is not None:\\n            children[n[\\\"parent\\\"]].append(n[\\\"id\\\"])\\n\\n    # Start with all nodes retained\\n    retained = set(n[\\\"id\\\"] for n in nodes)\\n\\n    # Process nodes deepest-first; stable tie-break by node ID ascending\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n[\\\"depth\\\"], n[\\\"id\\\"]))\\n\\n    removed_order = []\\n\\n    for node in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = node[\\\"id\\\"]\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: must be marked removable\\n        if not node[\\\"removable\\\"]:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: never remove the root (parent is None)\\n        if node[\\\"parent\\\"] is None:\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Hard constraint: never remove a node that still has retained children\\n        if any(c in retained for c in children[nid]):\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Tentative removal: check all required symbols remain represented\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        symbols_present = set(node_map[r][\\\"symbol\\\"] for r in retained)\\n        if not required_symbols.issubset(symbols_present):\\n            # Restore — removal would violate the interestingness oracle\\n            retained.add(nid)\\n            continue\\n\\n        # Removal is valid; record it\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"removed_node_ids\\\": removed_order,\\n        \\\"retained_node_ids\\\": retained_sorted\\n    }\\n\"}","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_01c5VP5pMob2c8cLIyBs5ivB","model":"claude-opus-4-7","role":"assistant","stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"type":"message","usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2515},"cache_creation_input_tokens":2515,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":75,"output_tokens":6121,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":5445},"service_tier":"standard"}}